% ── Abstract ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\begin{center}
\textbf{Abstract}
\end{center}

Climate change is usually framed as a change in where conditions are suitable. Where placement is constrained by something other than climate, it is better framed as a change in \textit{alignment}: a suitability window migrates while the constraint fixing where a use can go does not. We formalise adaptation space as $\mathcal{F}(\Delta T) = \mathcal{C}(\Delta T) \cap \mathcal{S}$, the intersection of a migrating suitability set with a stationary feasibility set, and show it contracts under warming even when neither set shrinks. The contraction is geometric: on a bounded landscape where area is unevenly distributed along the axis of migration, a sliding window loses more off its trailing edge than it gains at its leading edge, and loses the leading edge entirely at the boundary of $\mathcal{S}$. We demonstrate this on Hawaiian specialty coffee, where a designation of origin fixes $\mathcal{S}$ on geological timescales while $\mathcal{C}$ migrates on decadal ones. Between the 2035 and 2045 horizons the island-wide suitable area is static to within one percent---under the lower emissions pathway it grows---and the terrain footprint is unchanged, yet the intersection contracts 7--16\%, surviving every parameter setting tested. On the ground remaining, 72\% of Kona's and 77\% of Ka\okina u's mapped farm cells are on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035. A second constraint compounds the first: published degree-day development rates imply coffee berry borer generation capacity at the belt's upper margin rises by roughly a fifth to a third over the same interval, so the leading edge---the only direction adaptation can go---is itself deteriorating. Two screens moving on different timescales, not the size of any overestimation statistic, is what climate-only projections miss, and the configuration recurs wherever placement is fixed by geology, tenure, infrastructure, or designation.

