\section{Results}

\subsection{The Terrain Footprint Is Small and Does Not Move}
\label{sec:results:terrain}

The island-wide topographic identity map (Figure~\ref{fig:identity}) reveals the pre-existing structure of geographic constraint. Kona-like terrain (blue) forms a compact linear corridor on the west leeward flank at 150--950\,m; Ka\okina u-like terrain (red) concentrates on the south-facing terrace (185--895\,m) with limited extension onto adjacent elevated terrain. The combined topographic footprint covers only 14.8\% of island land (Kona 5.9\%, Ka\okina u 8.9\%); the remaining 85.2\% lacks the agro-ecological signature of either district. Because assignment is by nearer centroid subject to a distance cutoff, the two footprints are non-overlapping by construction and their sum is arithmetic, not an empirical finding.

What matters for what follows is not the size of this footprint but its fixity. Every feature defining it---elevation, elevation deviation, maximum slope, aspect, total and local relief, coastal distance---is a property of the volcano's constructional history, and none respond to a decadal warming signal. Mauna Loa's west flank will present the same slope, aspect and elevation band in 2045; what changes is which part of it is at the right temperature. Figure~\ref{fig:identity} is therefore the fixed domain into which every projection below must be read.

