\subsection{The Thermal Optimum Migrates Upslope (and What That Statistic Is Not)}
\label{sec:results:thermal}

Figure~\ref{fig:thermal} shows thermal suitability change across the full farmable island surface, evaluated by regional envelope without applying the terrain screen. Under the Kona envelope, 71.2\% of farmable cells gain thermal suitability by 2035 (69.8\% by 2045); under the Ka\okina u envelope, 69.8\% gain by 2035 (68.4\% by 2045). Because the two envelopes' optima are statistically indistinguishable (\S\ref{sec:studyarea}), the climate-only trajectories are nearly indistinguishable at the island-wide scale shown here---the districts diverge only once each is restricted to its own terrain footprint (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}).

These fractions are close to a statement about hypsometry rather than climate. Since $\Delta S_r > 0 \iff T < \mu_r - \Delta T_h/2$ and temperature within the belt is a function of elevation (\S\ref{sec:methods:thermal}), each is equivalent to an elevation contour: gain under the Kona envelope at 2035 means $z \gtrsim 583$\,m, and the two classifications agree on 99.9\% of farmable cells (Ka\okina u 2035 and Kona 2045 both $614$\,m, Ka\okina u 2045 $644$\,m; agreement $\geq$99.8\% throughout). ``Roughly 70\% of farmable land gains'' is, to within a percent, ``roughly 70\% of farmable land lies above $\approx$580--645\,m.'' We report it because it is what a climate-only assessment produces, and the contrast with the terrain-restricted result is the paper's subject.

