\subsection{What Remains: The Feasible Set Under Warming}
\label{sec:results:convergence}

Applying the topographic screen collapses the apparent thermal opportunity into a much smaller feasible domain, and the same pattern appears in both districts (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}): screened cells are predominantly declining at the lower, coastal-facing portion of the belt---where most existing farms sit---with a gaining fringe concentrated at the upper elevation margin. Overall, 69.0\% of Kona-like farmable terrain is on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035 (71.7\% by 2045) and 59.3\% of Ka\okina u-like terrain (63.9\%); the remainder gains, in that upper fringe.

Ka\okina u's declining majority is the smaller of the two, but it is the one result here that does not survive a change of distance metric: under the Mahalanobis screen the 2035 declining fractions fall to 57.7\% (Kona) and 50.3\% (Ka\okina u), making Ka\okina u a near-even split. The ordering survives---Kona's declining majority is the larger under either metric---but the margin does not. The farm-cell fractions are far less sensitive, since the two metrics exclude a different 5\% of cells but not a different part of the belt. We report the difference and do not build on it.

The central result concerns land that is actually farmed rather than land merely matching the terrain profile. Farms are not spread uniformly across their footprints---Kona's concentrate in the lower, warmer portion where the optimum departs first, and Ka\okina u's footprint (185--895\,m) extends both above and below its farms (252--740\,m)---so either way farm cells decline faster than the terrain containing them. On the 409 labelled Kona farm cells \textbf{72.1\%} are on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035 (95\% CI 65.8--78.0) and \textbf{78.5\%} by 2045 (72.6--84.9); for Ka\okina u's 62 cells, \textbf{77.4\%} (56.5--93.9) and \textbf{85.5\%} (65.0--98.8). Ka\okina u's intervals are wide because its cells form about eight independent spatial blocks; each replicate refits the standardiser, both centroids and the 95th-percentile distance cutoff, so the intervals carry threshold uncertainty rather than inheriting the point estimate (\S\ref{sec:methods:uncertainty}). Per emissions pathway the 2035 figures span 64--79\% and 69--86\%, identical in direction (Supplementary Table~S2). Weighting each cell by the coffee area it contains---cells differ in planted area, so naive acreage conversion is biased---gives 68.0\% and 81.8\% at 2035 and 74.7\% and 90.4\% at 2045, the two districts moving oppositely because Kona's larger cells sit higher in the belt (510 against 494\,m) and Ka\okina u's lower (519 against 524\,m). That is roughly 3{,}265 of Kona's $\approx$4{,}800 farmed acres and 982 of Ka\okina u's $\approx$1{,}200. Because cells are labelled by farm-centroid overlap, 32\% of mapped coffee acreage falls outside a labelled cell; those polygons are not elevation-biased (446\,m against 452\,m for captured polygons, Welch $p = 0.52$; 486 against 504\,m acre-weighted), so the omission is a grid-resolution artefact rather than a belt-edge one.

That upper fringe is the only direction the optimum is moving, and it does not extend further upslope: beyond it the slope steepens onto the volcano's upper constructional flanks, aspect and relief depart from the observed farm envelope, and substrate ages fall away. Warming does not open new coffee country; it relocates the favourable band within a fixed one, and the band is running out of footprint.

\subsection{The Contraction Mechanism: A Sliding Band on a Fixed Footprint}
\label{sec:results:contraction}

The terrain footprint is fixed (\S\ref{sec:results:terrain}) and the thermally favourable band moves (\S\ref{sec:results:thermal}). Put together, they give the paper's central claim, and the one result that survives every robustness check below.

Because temperature is effectively a function of elevation within the belt (\S\ref{sec:methods}), the mechanism admits an explicit one-dimensional statement. At $\tau = 0.5$ the level set is $|T + \Delta T - \mu_r| < \sqrt{2\ln 2}\,\sigma_r$, a band of fixed width in temperature and therefore---at $-5.72$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$---of fixed width in elevation: $\pm282$\,m for Kona, $\pm214$\,m for Ka\okina u. Warming translates it upslope by $\Delta T / 5.72$\,km without changing its width while the footprint stays put, so the feasible set is the overlap of a sliding interval with a fixed one. For Kona (footprint $150$--$950$\,m) the band runs $388$--$952$\,m at $+1.00$\,\textdegree C and $449$--$1{,}014$\,m at $+1.35$\,\textdegree C; clipped, the overlap falls from $562$ to $501$\,m, a predicted contraction of $10.9\%$. For Ka\okina u the same construction predicts $15.0\%$.

The full two-dimensional analysis returns $-15.5\%$ for Kona and $-12.4\%$ for Ka\okina u. Real terrain therefore contributes $-4.7$ percentage points for Kona and $+2.6$ for Ka\okina u, from hypsometry---on a shield volcano equal elevation intervals do not contain equal area---and from footprint shape. The interval model captures the sign and roughly two-thirds of the magnitude but \textit{reverses} the ordering between districts, which is why the two-dimensional computation is not redundant with it.

What happens between the two horizons states this most clearly. Island-wide, the thermally suitable area is essentially unchanged from 2035 to 2045---down 0.6\% under the Kona envelope, \textit{up} 0.5\% under Ka\okina u's---and the terrain set does not change at all. Yet their intersection contracts by 15.5\% (Kona, 1{,}371 to 1{,}158 cells) and 12.4\% (Ka\okina u, 1{,}839 to 1{,}611). Neither input shrinks; the intersection does, because the moving set is sliding off the stationary one. The pattern is starker under the lower pathway: under SSP2-4.5 the island-wide thermal set \textit{grows} between horizons ($+0.6\%$ Kona, $+1.1\%$ Ka\okina u) while the intersection contracts ($-7.9\%$, $-5.6\%$); under SSP5-8.5 the thermal set is flat and the intersection contracts far harder ($-17.4\%$, $-20.0\%$; Supplementary Table~S2). More thermally suitable land island-wide, less usable land inside the districts. Thermal opportunity elsewhere cannot compensate, because it is not on terrain that is Kona or Ka\okina u.

