\subsection{Why We Report the Multiplier Only as a Sign}
\label{sec:results:overestimation}

A single ratio---thermally suitable area over terrain-constrained suitable area---is the obvious summary statistic, and we report it for completeness because a reader will otherwise compute it. It should not be read as a measurement. At $\tau = 0.5$ and the 95th-percentile threshold it is $6.9\times$ for Kona (95\% CI 6.3--9.1) and $3.9\times$ for Ka\okina u (3.2--8.6), but it spans $3.2\times$--$20.7\times$ for Ka\okina u across defensible threshold and distance-metric choices (Supplementary Table~S1), and per scenario runs $6.1$--$8.3\times$ and $3.5$--$4.6\times$ (Supplementary Table~S2).

Two results explain why. First, the ratio decomposes into a suitability rate times the inverse terrain fraction: $6.9 = 0.468 \times 14.7$ for Kona and $3.9 = 0.342 \times 11.3$ for Ka\okina u, so most of its magnitude is the terrain fraction---an input from the farm polygons, not a finding. Second, a null distribution of 2{,}000 draws stratified to reproduce each district's elevation histogram---farmable cells drawn bin by bin to match that histogram exactly, everything else randomised---reproduces the observed value to within two parts in a thousand: Kona $6.87\times$ against a null median of $6.88\times$, Ka\okina u $3.88\times$ against $3.88\times$. Since temperature within the belt is a function of elevation to within $0.032$\,\textdegree C, that near-identity is close to a restatement of the same fact. The multiplier measures the elevation distribution of whatever terrain is screened, not a property of Kona or Ka\okina u, and an earlier reading of a size-matched null as evidence that these districts are unusually well sited does not survive the elevation-matched construction.

Matching shape as well as size changes nothing. Kona's footprint is a $3.8{:}1$ corridor and Ka\okina u's a $1.7{:}1$ terrace; drawing 2{,}000 patches of the same area \textit{and} the same elongation, at random locations and bearings, puts the observed multiplier at the low edge of the null rather than above it (Kona $6.87\times$ against a shape-matched null median of $14.9\times$, exceeded by 95\% of draws; Ka\okina u $3.88\times$ against $10.1\times$, exceeded by 100\%). Random patches score \textit{worse} because they land on elevation distributions that miss the suitable band. Neither the districts' size, their shape, nor their placement makes the multiplier what it is---their elevation histogram does.

The district difference is likewise a width effect rather than a position effect. At $\tau = 0.5$ the level set spans $\pm 1.1774\,\sigma_r$, which is $1.61$\,\textdegree C for Kona against $1.22$\,\textdegree C for Ka\okina u; that ratio of $1.32$ matches the ratio of exceedance rates ($26.1/19.8 = 1.32$) to three digits, while the $0.18$\,\textdegree C difference in $\mu_r$ is worth about $32$\,m of elevation and contributes almost nothing.

% ── 4. Discussion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Discussion}

A thermal optimum that migrates upslope is only an adaptation opportunity if there is upslope land the crop can occupy. For a terroir-bound crop there generally is not, because the terrain configuration defining the product is fixed on a timescale orders of magnitude longer than the one on which the optimum moves. Nothing shrank except the alignment between a set that moves and a set that cannot.

Wherever cultivable terrain is spatially structured---confined to specific soils, aspects, parent materials or slope configurations---climate-only projections will overstate accessible adaptation space, as previous work has noted without quantifying \citep{Bunn2015, OvalleRivera2015}. The degree of overestimation scales with the geographic specificity of the terrain requirement, which in orographic specialty crop systems is extreme. Both districts face the same version of this: their thermal optima are indistinguishable, so a shared warming signal produces nearly identical island-wide trajectories, and what differs is a terrain geometry that in neither case leaves room to expand. The thermal window shifts for both; the geomorphological door widens for neither.

The convergence map (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}) translates this into planning terms. For both districts the priority is thermal resilience inside the existing footprint---shade management, higher-tolerance variety trials, and monitoring at the upper fringe where the intersection remains positive through 2035--2045. The map identifies those fringe cells explicitly, as priority sites for agronomic intervention rather than confirmed relocation targets (\S\ref{sec:discussion:limits}). Non-farmed, topographically matched, thermally-gaining cells are candidates for feasibility studies only after terrain, soil and access screening---thermal trajectory second, not first.

The economic stakes follow from the geometry and fall on existing production. Roughly 72\% of Kona's and 77\% of Ka\okina u's farm cells are on a declining trajectory by 2035, area-weighted to 68\% and 82\%, against a 2021--22 crop valued at $\approx$\$113 million green \citep{Lee2023}. Because ``100\% Kona'' certification prices geographic identity, resilience in place---shade management, heat-tolerant varieties---is the primary option rather than one of several; relocation is constrained by the same fixed footprint that produces the contraction. A planner reading the unscreened projection sees roughly 70\% of farmable island land improving, which is a statement about the island's hypsometry rather than about accessible ground.

One biotic constraint lies outside this framework and acts specifically on the terrain the analysis identifies as gaining. Coffee berry borer (\textit{Hypothenemus hampei}) reached Kona in September 2010 and Ka\okina u in May 2011, and in the two following seasons was associated with an estimated \$25.7 million in lost sales and a statewide yield decline near 20\% \citep{Lee2023}. \citet{Hamilton2019} estimate its development on Hawai\okina i Island at $332$ degree-days per generation above a lower threshold of $14.9$\,\textdegree C. Applying that model to the temperature surface used here---rather than to their fitted generations-versus-elevation regression, which would require extrapolating past its 778\,m upper sampling limit---reproduces their observed field rates across the belt (0.71 generations per month at 204\,m falling to 0.41 at 778\,m, against their observed range of 0.33--0.78) and puts 2.5--3.1 generations in a July--December berry window at 600--780\,m, inside the 2.11--3.27 they report. Under the increments used here the same model implies generation-capacity increases of roughly 18\% at 600\,m and 23\% at 800\,m by 2035, and 25\% and 31\% by 2045. The proportional increase is largest where the current rate is lowest---the upper margin this analysis identifies as the only gaining terrain. The fringe that gains thermal suitability gains pest pressure fastest, and one warming signal drives both.

Coffee leaf rust (\textit{Hemileia vastatrix}) is a differently structured omission. Confirmed on Maui on 30 October 2020---the first United States detection---it was present on all major Hawaiian islands within nine months \citep{HDOA2021CLR}, postdating the baseline period used here. Its infection requirement is free moisture on the leaf rather than a temperature threshold, and this analysis does not project precipitation, so it sits outside the model in a way no thermal screen can capture; the upper fringe's higher cloud and moisture exposure is a plausible aggravating factor rather than a mitigating one.

Neither constraint undermines the structural argument---a fixed footprint still cannot track a moving optimum---but together they bound how the gaining fringe should be read: as \textit{thermally} favourable terrain, not as an agronomic refuge. Joint modelling of pest and pathogen pressure alongside the two screens is the single most valuable extension of this work.

\label{sec:discussion:limits}The most consequential limitation is quantified in \S\ref{sec:methods:thermal}: within the belt the temperature field is a function of elevation to within $0.032$\,\textdegree C, and the terrain screen uses elevation, so the two screens are not independent. Everything downstream inherits this. The finding that the gaining fraction concentrates at the upper margin cannot be separated from the fact that moving upslope also moves a cell away from its district's elevation centroid: the analysis can show that the favourable band migrates while the envelope does not, but not that any particular upper-fringe cell is a genuine refuge. Resolving that needs a screen built on non-elevation descriptors, or a thermal field with more non-elevation structure than $0.032$\,\textdegree C. Precipitation is not projected independently, and Hawaiian precipitation projections carry wider model spread than temperature. The screen contains no hydrological, tenure or access variable, so cells passing it may be unavailable for reasons outside the model. None of this alters the geometry; it bounds how precisely the result can be located.

The configuration generalises. Write $\mathcal{F}(\Delta T) = \mathcal{C}(\Delta T) \cap \mathcal{S}$, with $\mathcal{S}$ any constraint on where a use can physically be placed. Three conditions suffice for the contraction reported here: $\mathcal{C}$ migrates along some axis, $\mathcal{S}$ is fixed on a longer timescale than that migration, and area is unevenly distributed along the axis within $\mathcal{S}$. Nothing in that list is specific to temperature, elevation, or coffee. Designation-of-origin viticulture satisfies it with geology as $\mathcal{S}$; conservation reserves with tenure and reserve boundaries; irrigated systems with water conveyance; forestry with rotation length against soil and tenure; any fixed infrastructure sited for a climate envelope with its own footprint. In each case the quantity of interest is not how much land becomes climatically suitable but how long the window and the constraint stay in register, and what happens at the leading edge when they cease to. Coffee is a useful demonstration because both sets are independently measurable and a designation of origin makes $\mathcal{S}$ unusually explicit---written into the product's definition rather than inferred. Ovalle-Rivera et al.\ \citep{OvalleRivera2015} project Colombian Arabica shifting 500--1{,}300\,m upslope and note that converting that terrain is uncertain on soil and access grounds: the same configuration with $\mathcal{S}$ unmeasured. Ethiopian highland Arabica on Afromontane terrain is another \citep{Davis2012}.

% ── 5. Conclusions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Conclusions}

Terrain is fixed on geological timescales; a thermal optimum moves on decadal ones. Where a crop's identity---and its price---is bound to a particular terrain configuration, that mismatch means the optimum can migrate but the crop cannot follow it.

Hawaiian coffee makes the mismatch legible because both sides can be measured. Only 5.9\% of the Big Island carries Kona's terrain signature and 8.9\% carries Ka\okina u's, and those footprints belong to the volcano, not the climate. Against them the thermally suitable area island-wide is static between 2035 and 2045---down 0.6\% under one envelope, up 0.5\% under the other's---while the intersection contracts by 15.5\% and 12.4\%, produced entirely by the two sets sliding out of register. The loss falls hardest on land already in production. A climate-only reading of the same warming would report roughly 70\% of farmable island land improving, a figure very nearly a restatement of how much of the island lies above 600\,m.

The convergence map (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}) is the resulting decision surface---not where coffee might theoretically exist under future temperatures, but where it can. Both districts are constrained in the same way and to a similar degree: one problem with two local expressions, not two problems.

Under warming the question is not how much land becomes climatically suitable, but how long the suitable band and the constraint that fixes placement remain in register, and what happens when they separate. That is a question about the intersection of a moving set with a fixed one, and a thermal projection alone cannot answer it.

