% ── 6. Methods ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Data}
\label{sec:data}

\subsubsection{Geospatial Grid and Farm Boundaries}

Farm boundaries were sourced from the 2020 Hawai\okina i Statewide Agricultural Land Use Baseline \citep{PerroyCollier2021} (674 coffee polygons, $\approx$6{,}000\,acres total), projected to UTM Zone~4N and intersected with a 500\,m grid to yield 471 grid cells labelled by farm-centroid overlap. All 471 cells (Kona $n = 409$, Ka\okina u $n = 62$) are used throughout the analysis below. The two quantities estimated from the farm sample---the topographic centroids (\S\ref{sec:methods:topo}) and the thermal envelope parameters (\S\ref{sec:methods:thermal})---require only each cell's terrain features, sampled from the island DEM, and its temperature, sampled from the gridded climatology; both are available for all 471 cells.

No cell is excluded for missing SSURGO texture (which enters no feature set used here) or for missing ERA5-Land extraction. The latter matters: ERA5-Land's $\approx$9\,km mask does not resolve the Kona coastal strip, and the 86 affected cells returned no data in any of 108 monthly composites. They are not a random subset but the low, warm seaward margin of the belt (327\,m and 21.9\,\textdegree C against 535\,m and 20.8\,\textdegree C for the rest; $p<10^{-5}$)---the population most relevant to where farms sit relative to their optimum. The 250\,m climatology resolves them, and ERA5-Land now supplies only an island-wide $\Delta T$.

\subsubsection{Terrain Features}

Elevation data from the USGS 3D Elevation Program (3DEP) 1/3 arc-second DEM \citep{USGS3DEP} were mosaic-merged across three tiles and average-resampled to 500\,m. Eight topographic features were computed per cell: mean elevation, elevation standard deviation, maximum slope, aspect sine, aspect cosine, total relief, local relief, and distance to coastline.

\subsubsection{Climate}

Baseline climate was sourced from ERA5-Land daily reanalysis \citep{HersbachERA5}, aggregated to 18 annual and seasonal features for 2017--2025. Future projections used delta-method bias correction applied to ACCESS-CM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-HR under SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5, obtained from the NASA NEX-GDDP-CMIP6 archive \citep{Thrasher2022}, for 20-year windows centred on 2035 and 2045. Model-ensemble means within each scenario are $\Delta T_{2035} = +0.60$\,\textdegree C and $\Delta T_{2045} = +0.92$\,\textdegree C under SSP2-4.5, and $+1.41$ and $+1.78$\,\textdegree C under SSP5-8.5.

SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5 are alternative forcing pathways, not ensemble members, so all headline quantities are reported per scenario (Supplementary Table~S2), with model-ensemble averaging retained within each. Maps are shown at reference increments of $+1.00$ and $+1.35$\,\textdegree C, which bracket the 2035 and 2045 scenario range.

$\Delta T$ is applied uniformly in space, a constraint of the source data: NEX-GDDP-CMIP6 is distributed on a $0.25$\textdegree\ grid on which the whole island falls within essentially one cell, and the extracted $\Delta T$ is identical to four decimal places across all farm cells. Uniformity still warrants scrutiny, since elevation-dependent warming is documented in Hawai\okina i---but its strongest signal sits at the trade-wind inversion near $2{,}150$--$2{,}225$\,m \citep{KagawaVivianiGiambelluca2020}, well above a 200--950\,m belt. Imposing $\Delta T(z) = \Delta T_0 + g(z-\bar{z})$ and sweeping $g$ from 0 to $2$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$ steepens the contraction monotonically (Kona $-15.5$ to $-22.3\%$, Ka\okina u $-12.4$ to $-22.8\%$), so uniform $\Delta T$ is the conservative assumption.

\subsubsection{Surficial Geology}
\label{sec:data:geology}

Lava-flow age was sourced from the digital geologic database of the Island of Hawai\okina i \citep{Trusdell2006}, which digitises the underlying 1:100{,}000 geologic map of Wolfe and Morris (USGS Geologic Investigations Series I-2524-A, 1996); we used the National Park Service Geologic Resources Inventory shapefile reformatting of that database, comprising 6{,}141 mapped rock-unit polygons joined to their legend entries by unit symbol. Polygons mapped as ``Historic A.D.\ 1790 or younger''---the youngest, least-weathered substrate, including the 1859 and 1950 Mauna Loa flows and the bulk of Kīlauea's recent output---were rasterised onto the same 500\,m UTM grid as the island DEM (\S\ref{sec:methods:farmability}).

\subsection{Topographic Feasibility Screen}
\label{sec:methods:topo}

Eight topographic features (\S\ref{sec:data}) were standardised using a \texttt{StandardScaler} fitted on the 471 labelled farm cells, and regional centroids were computed as the mean standardised feature vector for each district. The feature set---elevation mean, elevation standard deviation, maximum slope, aspect sine, aspect cosine, total relief, local relief, and coastline distance---captures the primary axes of topographic variation that distinguish cultivated terrain on the Big Island: elevation band, slope, aspect orientation, landscape roughness, and coastal proximity. For each island cell, Euclidean distance to each centroid was converted to a continuous similarity score:
\[
    s_r = \frac{1}{1 + d_r}, \quad r \in \{\text{Kona},\; \text{Ka\textquoteleft u}\}
\]
A topographic identity score $\text{identity} = s_{\text{Ka'u}} - s_{\text{Kona}}$ assigns each cell to the closer regional profile: positive values indicate Ka\okina u-like terrain, negative values indicate Kona-like terrain. Because $s_r = 1/(1+d_r)$ is monotone in $d_r$, this score changes no assignment that the raw distances would not also make; only its sign is interpreted. Its magnitude is a compressed, non-linear function of a distance difference and carries no units, so the numerical values on the Figure~\ref{fig:identity} colour scale should be read as an ordering rather than as a measured quantity.

Euclidean distance is used because the objective is domain bounding, not probabilistic classification: the screen asks whether a cell falls inside the cloud of observed farm terrain. Standardisation removes scale differences; the remaining correlation structure is treated as co-occurring terrain constraints rather than redundancy to be decorrelated. Repeating the analysis under Mahalanobis distance with a pooled within-class covariance gives a smaller Ka\okina u footprint (3.72 vs.\ 8.90\% of island land) and, at the baseline threshold, a larger multiplier (11.3$\times$ vs.\ 3.9$\times$); the two converge as the threshold widens (Supplementary Table~S1). Kona is barely affected either way---its footprint moves 5.95 to 6.28\% and its multiplier 6.9 to 7.3$\times$---so the metric sensitivity is specific to Ka\okina u's more correlated feature set. Both agree that terrain-constrained suitability is several-fold smaller than thermal-only suitability at every threshold, which is the only conclusion we draw from either.

This defines a feasibility constraint: terrain unlike existing farms is excluded from suitability scoring regardless of thermal conditions. The screen captures realized agro-ecological structure, not the full fundamental niche, and is intended as a constraint on economically and operationally viable expansion, not biological possibility. The framework is not a predictive classifier of suitability, but a constraint on the geographic domain over which suitability can be meaningfully evaluated. Stability of the classification under threshold perturbation (Supplementary Fig.~S1) is a robustness check, not a validation: it establishes that the screen is insensitive to the cutoff, not that it identifies terrain capable of supporting coffee. An out-of-sample test is reported in \S\ref{sec:methods:kauai}.

Cells whose distance to the nearer centroid exceeds the 95th percentile of within-region farm-to-centroid distances are excluded, a boundary of observed agro-ecological coherence rather than a statistical cutoff. Across a 90th--97.5th percentile sweep the footprint stays small (5.0--11.0\% of island land classifying as Ka\okina u-like) and Jaccard similarity to the baseline stays above 0.79 except at the 90th percentile, where Ka\okina u---whose centroid rests on 62 cells---destabilises (Supplementary Fig.~S1, Table~S1). At the baseline, 5.9\% of island land classifies as Kona-like and 8.9\% as Ka\okina u-like; the remaining 85.2\% shares neither signature.

\subsection{Out-of-Sample Behaviour}
\label{sec:methods:kauai}

Two tests bound how far the screen transfers. Applied unchanged to Kaua\okina i --- same DEM product, grid and features, an island never seen in fitting --- it classifies 224 of 5{,}986 land cells and recovers \textit{none} of the 61 cells containing mapped coffee. The failure is substantive: Kaua\okina i's coffee sits at 84\,m on 7\textdegree\ slopes against Kona's 494\,m and 19\textdegree. The screen identifies Kona- and Ka\okina u-like terrain, not coffee-growing terrain, which is the expected behaviour of an identity-anchored screen rather than an arability mask, and is evidence that ``coffee country'' is not one terrain signature.

The claims in this paper depend instead on generalising to Kona-like ground not fitted on. Holding out contiguous latitude bands of the belt and refitting standardisation, centroids and threshold on the remainder recovers a mean 86\% of held-out farm cells against a 94.9\% in-sample reference --- but unevenly, running 100, 38, 94, 100 and 100\% across five folds. The screen extends along the belt on average, not uniformly, so nominations of specific upper-fringe cells (\S\ref{sec:results:convergence}) are not uniform-confidence predictions. Both tests are detailed in Supplementary Note~S2.

\subsection{Thermal Envelope Model}
\label{sec:methods:thermal}

Island-wide temperature comes from the Kodama et al.\ \citep{Kodama2024} Hawai\okina i climate atlas, a 250\,m gridded monthly product built from the Hawai\okina i Climate Data Portal's spatial modelling framework. We averaged all 384 monthly mean-temperature rasters (1990--2021) into one climatological mean and reprojected it by bilinear resampling to the same 500\,m UTM Zone~4N grid as the DEM, so the island-wide surface and the farm-cell temperatures come from one grid at one scale. A regression fit to the farm cells themselves is unusable: elevation does not predict farm-cell temperature within either district in isolation ($R^2 \approx 0.01$ against ERA5-Land), and ERA5-Land's 9\,km grid cannot resolve a within-district gradient. Over island land the climatology spans 5.6--23.8\,\textdegree C, consistent with the $\approx$22--24\,\textdegree C statewide sea-level mean and the $\approx-6$ to $-6.5$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$ lapse reported below the trade-wind inversion \citep{KagawaVivianiGiambelluca2020, GiambellucaNullet1991}.

Separate Gaussian thermal envelopes approximate each district's thermal niche, consistent with unimodal species--environment response assumptions widely used in ecological modelling \citep{Guisan2000}:
\[
    S_r(T,\,\Delta T) = \exp\!\left[-\frac{1}{2}
    \left(\frac{T + \Delta T - \mu_r}{\sigma_r}\right)^2\right]
\]
where $\mu_r$ and $\sigma_r$ are the mean and $1.5\times$ the standard deviation of that climatology sampled at each farm cell's own location across the 471 labelled farm cells ($\mu_{\text{Kona}}=21.00$\,\textdegree C, $\sigma_{\text{Kona}}=1.37$\,\textdegree C, Kona $n = 409$; $\mu_{\text{Ka'u}}=20.82$\,\textdegree C, $\sigma_{\text{Ka'u}}=1.04$\,\textdegree C, $n=62$). The $1.5\times$ inflation stands in for farm-scale microclimatic variability---shade, canopy, very local aspect---unresolved at 500\,m. It is a judgement, not a calibrated quantity, so we report its consequences: it changes no gain/loss count at all (see below), and moves the 2035 multiplier only from 6.9 to 7.1$\times$ (Kona) and 3.6 to 4.2$\times$ (Ka\okina u) across $1.0$--$2.0\times$, less than either the terrain threshold or the distance metric moves it. One thermal model, one temperature scale, both districts, both the constrained and unconstrained analyses.

Two properties of this construction govern how its outputs read. First, for a Gaussian envelope $\sigma_r$ cancels out of the \textit{sign} of the classification: $\Delta S_r(h) > 0 \iff T < \mu_r - \Delta T_h/2$, independent of $\sigma_r$, for any $T$. It sets magnitudes---the level set $\mathcal{C}_r$, the colour scales---but never which cells gain or lose. Second, the thermal and terrain screens are not independent, and less so at this scale than an island-wide statistic implies. Regressed on elevation across all island land the climatology gives $R^2 = 0.98$ (implied lapse $-5.05$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$, residual SD $0.60$\,\textdegree C), but that residual is curvature across the full $0$--$4{,}166$\,m span, nearly all of it above the coffee belt. Restricted to the $150$--$950$\,m band the districts occupy, the fit is $R^2 = 0.999$, slope $-5.721$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$, residual SD $0.032$\,\textdegree C. This within-belt slope is what converts every temperature threshold to an elevation, since every such boundary lies inside the belt. Aspect explains under 1\% of the residual, which is 43$\times$ smaller than $\sigma_{\text{Kona}}$ and 31$\times$ smaller than $\Delta T_{2035}$.

This is a property of the product. The Kodama climatology is built by optimised piecewise-linear regression on elevation \citep{Kodama2024}, so within one narrow band it cannot carry information independent of elevation. Adopting it replaced an \textit{assumed} elevation dependence with an \textit{observed} one---lapse, intercept and curvature are now measured---but it did not make the two screens independent, and no gridded product built this way could. Within the belt, temperature is elevation. The boundary therefore stays close to a single isotherm and hence a single contour, and the two districts' near-identical island-wide gain fractions follow directly from their near-identical $\mu_r$.

Thermal suitability change at horizon $h$ is:
\[
    \Delta S_r(h) = S_r(T,\,\Delta T_h) - S_r(T,\,0)
\]
Positive $\Delta S$ indicates warming toward greater alignment with the thermal optimum; negative values indicate reduced alignment.

One consequence bounds what the spatial analysis can claim. Because $\mu_r$ is the mean of the same distribution against which $\Delta S_r$ is evaluated, the declining fraction is approximately $\Phi(\Delta T_h / 2 s_r)$ for uninflated $s_r$: for Kona at 2035 the closed form gives 70.8\% against an observed 72.1\%, for Ka\okina u 76.5\% against 77.4\%. The statistic is largely the ratio of warming to within-district temperature spread. What the gridded analysis adds is spatial: which cells decline, whether they coincide with farmed land, and whether improving cells fall inside the footprint.

Two quantities use this score and are kept separate. The \textit{direction-of-change} statistic $\Delta S_r(h)$ classifies each cell as gaining or losing under warming irrespective of its absolute level. The \textit{thermal suitability set} is a level set, $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T) = \{g \in \mathcal{G} : S_r(T_g, \Delta T) > \tau\}$: farmable cells whose absolute future suitability exceeds $\tau$, gain or loss. Only $\mathcal{C}_r$ defines the multiplier, because a cold high cell can register $\Delta S_r > 0$ while warming from a negligible score to a still-negligible one, and counting that as opportunity would inflate the thermal-only set with land never viable. We use $\tau = 0.5$, with sensitivity over $0.1$--$0.8$ reported in \S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}.

\subsection{Farmability Screen}
\label{sec:methods:farmability}

All projections are restricted to farmable terrain: maximum slope $\leq 25$\textdegree, excluding cliff margins, \textit{and} not mapped as a lava flow of Historic (A.D.\ 1790 or younger) age (\S\ref{sec:data:geology})---slope alone does not exclude young, barren, unconsolidated lava fields, which can be flat enough to pass a slope-only screen while remaining agriculturally unusable. Of island land, 84.8\% satisfies both criteria: 94.3\% passes the slope criterion alone, and the historic-lava exclusion removes a further 9.5 percentage points (4{,}044 cells; a further 149 historic-lava cells sit on terrain already excluded as too steep, so the two criteria overlap slightly and 9.9\% of island land is historic lava in total). The excluded flows are concentrated on Mauna Loa's southwest rift and Kīlauea's east and southwest rift zones.

\subsection{Uncertainty Quantification}
\label{sec:methods:uncertainty}

Farm cells are spatially contiguous, so intervals are spatial block bootstraps (blocks of $\approx$8 cells by $k$-means on projected coordinates; 2{,}000 replicates; the whole screen refitted within each replicate for the factor). Block intervals are 1.8--2.4$\times$ wider than independent resampling, which is anticonservative here. Construction and diagnostics are in Supplementary Note~S1.

\subsection{Terrain-Thermal Convergence Analysis}

The terrain-thermal convergence map is the study's primary analytical product (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}). For each farmable cell, thermal suitability change is displayed only if the cell passes the topographic feasibility screen, and is colour-coded by the matched regional envelope: Kona-like cells receive $\Delta S_{\text{Kona}}$; Ka\okina u-like cells receive $\Delta S_{\text{Ka'u}}$. Farmable cells outside both profiles are shown in mid-grey---these cells may experience thermally favourable futures but lack the terrain, soil, and microclimate structure of existing coffee country. Steep terrain appears in light grey. The coloured footprint is the only geographic space where thermal adaptation signals are simultaneously grounded in terrain reality.

